from . import n
n("projectile_motion","Projectiles and free fall","1",(1e-2,1e5),"C1","Classical mechanics",
 "Thrown objects follow curved paths because gravity pulls them down while their sideways motion continues. Without air, all objects fall equally fast.",
 "Horizontal and vertical motion are independent. Air drag adds a terminal speed, which is why raindrops do not hit like bullets.",
 "y=v₀t sinθ−½gt²; range=v₀² sin2θ/g; terminal v_t=√(2mg/ρC_dA).",
 "Uniform g and no spin or drag assumed.",
 em="newton_laws gravitation_newton",ev="Galileo's inclined planes|Apollo 15 hammer-feather drop",refs="UP1 4; HSP 5",sim="human")
n("rocketry","Rockets and spaceflight","7",(1,1e12),"C1","Engineering",
 "Rockets move by throwing mass backwards, and the Tsiolkovsky rocket equation says how much fuel it takes to change speed.",
 "Most of a rocket is propellant. Orbiting needs about 7.8 km/s sideways at low Earth altitude, not just height.",
 "Δv=v_e ln(m₀/m_f); v_circ=√(GM/r)≈7.8 km/s (LEO); v_esc(Earth)=11.2 km/s.",
 "Chemical rockets limited by exhaust speed ≈ 3–4.5 km/s.",
 em="conservation_momentum orbits_kepler",ev="Apollo, Voyager and satellite launches",refs="UP1 9; AST 3",sim="solarsystem")
# ---------- relativity ----------
n("special_relativity","Special relativity","1",(1e-15,1e26),"C1","Relativity",
 "The speed of light is the same for every observer, so time and space measurements depend on motion. Moving clocks run slow and length contracts.",
 "Mass and energy are interchangeable, and nothing with mass can reach light speed. The theory combines space and time into one four-dimensional spacetime.",
 "γ=1/√(1−v²/c²); Δt=γΔt₀; E²=(pc)²+(mc²)²; E=mc²; 1 u=931.494 MeV/c².",
 "Valid in flat spacetime (no gravity); exact otherwise at all tested speeds.",
 em="maxwell noether",ev="Muon lifetime extension|Michelson-Morley|Particle accelerators",refs="UP3 5",ex="general_relativity qed nuclear_binding gps_relativity")
n("general_relativity","General relativity","1",(1e3,1e26),"C1","Relativity",
 "Gravity is the curvature of spacetime caused by mass and energy, and objects follow the straightest paths through that curved geometry. It extends Newton's gravity to strong fields and cosmic distances.",
 "Its predictions include light bending, gravitational time dilation, gravitational waves and black holes. All tests so far have confirmed it.",
 "G_μν+Λg_μν=(8πG/c⁴)T_μν; Schwarzschild r_s=2GM/c²; Mercury precession 42.98″/century.",
 "Tested from lab to cosmological scales; must fail at singularities and the Planck scale.",
 em="special_relativity diff_geometry gravitation_newton",ev="Eddington 1919 eclipse|Hulse-Taylor pulsar|LIGO 2015|Event Horizon Telescope images",refs="AST 24; UP3 5",ex="black_holes gravitational_waves big_bang gps_relativity gravitational_lensing",sim="blackhole",
 fr="Singularities signal where GR breaks down~open")
n("gps_relativity","Relativity in GPS","7",(2e7,2e7),"C1","Technology",
 "GPS satellite clocks run at different rates than ground clocks, and the system would drift by kilometres a day if it ignored relativity.",
 "Speed slows the clocks by about 7 microseconds per day while weaker gravity speeds them up by about 45, a net gain of about 38.",
 "Δt_total≈+45.7−7.2≈+38.5 μs/day; orbital radius ≈26 560 km.",
 "Small corrections; classical models fail only through accumulated drift.",
 em="special_relativity general_relativity",ev="GPS operation depends on it",refs="UP3 5")
n("gravitational_lensing","Gravitational lensing","10",(1e16,1e24),"C1","Relativity",
 "Mass bends the path of light passing near it, so galaxies and clusters can magnify and distort the images of objects behind them.",
 "Lensing measures mass whether or not it shines, which is a key piece of the dark matter evidence.",
 "α=4GM/(c²b); Einstein radius θ_E=√(4GM D_ds/(c²D_dD_s)).",
 "Weak-field limit of GR.",
 em="general_relativity",ev="Einstein cross|Bullet cluster mass map",refs="AST 28; AST 24",ex="dark_matter_evidence")
n("gravitational_waves","Gravitational waves","1",(1e3,1e22),"C1","Relativity",
 "Accelerating masses ripple spacetime. Detectors like LIGO sense stretches smaller than a proton in arms several kilometres long.",
 "The first detected merger (2015) matched GR waveforms for two ~30 solar-mass black holes. Mergers of neutron stars also emit light, enabling multi-messenger astronomy.",
 "h≈ΔL/L∼10⁻²¹; v=c; chirp mass ℳ=(m₁m₂)^{3/5}/(m₁+m₂)^{1/5}; L_GW ∼ c⁵/G for equal mergers.",
 "Linear weak-field regime for the propagation; strong-field merger needs numerical relativity.",
 em="general_relativity",ev="GW150914|GW170817 with kilonova|Hulse-Taylor orbital decay",refs="AST 24",fref="gw250114",ex="kilonova_rprocess",sim="blackhole")
n("black_holes","Black holes","10",(1e3,1e13),"C1","Relativity",
 "A black hole is a region where gravity is so strong that nothing, not even light, can leave. They form when massive stars collapse and also sit at the centres of galaxies.",
 "The edge, the event horizon, is not a surface but a one-way boundary. Black holes are described by mass, spin and charge, and spin lets them power jets.",
 "r_s=2GM/c²≈2.95 km (M/M☉); T_H=ħc³/(8πGMk_B)≈6×10⁻⁸ K (M☉/M); S=k_Bc³A/(4Għ).",
 "Hawking radiation is predicted but unobserved; interior and information fate depend on quantum gravity.",
 em="general_relativity stellar_death",ev="Cygnus X-1|Sgr A* orbits|EHT M87 and Sgr A* images|LIGO mergers",refs="AST 24; AST 27",ex="agn_quasars smbh_growth",sim="blackhole sgrA",
 fr="Hawking radiation unobserved~open")
# ---------- QM ----------
n("quantum_discovery","Origins of quantum theory","1",(1e-10,1e-6),"C1","Quantum mechanics",
 "Around 1900 blackbody light, the photoelectric effect and atomic spectra could not be explained by classical physics. Planck and Einstein proposed that energy comes in discrete packets.",
 "Bohr's atom, de Broglie's matter waves and then Heisenberg and Schrödinger's mechanics replaced the old picture by 1926.",
 "E=hf; photoelectric K_max=hf−φ; de Broglie λ=h/p.",
 "Historical; modern theory supersedes the 'old quantum theory'.",
 em="blackbody",ev="Photoelectric effect|Compton scattering|Atomic spectra",refs="UP3 6",ex="wave_particle")
n("wave_particle","Wave-particle duality","1",(1e-15,1e-5),"C1","Quantum mechanics",
 "Light and matter show both wave and particle behaviour: electrons make interference patterns but are always detected one at a time.",
 "Each particle is described by a wave of probability. The pattern builds up from single events even when particles pass through the apparatus one at a time.",
 "λ=h/p; electron at 100 eV: λ≈0.12 nm; two-slit fringe spacing Δy=λL/d.",
 "Universal; wave behaviour only observable when λ is comparable to apparatus features.",
 em="wave_optics quantum_discovery",ev="Davisson-Germer|Single-electron double slit|C60 interference",refs="UP3 6; UP3 7",ex="schrodinger uncertainty")
n("schrodinger","Schrödinger equation and the wave function","1",(1e-15,1e-6),"C1","Quantum mechanics",
 "The Schrödinger equation says how a quantum system's wave function changes in time. The square of the wave function gives the probability of finding the particle.",
 "Solving it for an electron near a nucleus gives the allowed energy levels of atoms. Bound states are quantised because waves must fit in the space available.",
 "iħ∂ψ/∂t=Ĥψ; Ĥ=−ħ²∇²/2m+V; P=|ψ|²; ħ=1.0546×10⁻³⁴ J s; particle in a box E_n=n²π²ħ²/2mL².",
 "Non-relativistic, single-particle form; QFT handles creation of particles.",
 em="linalg_hilbert lagrangian wave_particle",ev="Hydrogen spectrum to <1 ppm|Chemistry|Semiconductor devices",refs="UP3 7",ex="quantum_orbitals tunneling band_theory")
n("uncertainty","Uncertainty principle","1",(1e-15,1e-6),"C1","Quantum mechanics",
 "You cannot know both a particle's position and momentum exactly at the same time. This is a property of nature, not a limit of instruments.",
 "It is why electrons do not fall into the nucleus and why a confined particle has minimum motion (zero-point energy).",
 "ΔxΔp≥ħ/2; ΔEΔt≳ħ/2; zero-point energy E₀=½ħω.",
 "Universal; follows from wave nature (Fourier).",
 em="fourier schrodinger",ev="Neutron diffraction|Casimir effect",refs="UP3 7")
n("spin_pauli","Spin and the Pauli exclusion principle","1",(1e-15,1e-9),"C1","Quantum mechanics",
 "Particles carry an intrinsic angular momentum called spin. Two identical electrons cannot share the same quantum state, which forces atoms into shells.",
 "Exclusion makes matter occupy space and holds up white dwarfs and neutron stars. Spin-statistics theorem sorts particles into fermions and bosons.",
 "s=½ (electron), g≈2.00232; fermions antisymmetric ψ(1,2)=−ψ(2,1); Fermi energy E_F=(ħ²/2m)(3π²n)^{2/3}.",
 "Exact for identical particles; spin-statistics proven in relativistic QFT.",
 em="schrodinger standard_model",ev="Stern-Gerlach|Periodic table|White dwarf mass limit",refs="UP3 8",ex="periodic_table stellar_death")
n("entanglement","Entanglement and Bell tests","1",(1e-15,1e5),"C1","Quantum mechanics",
 "Two particles can share one quantum state so that measuring one instantly fixes correlations with the other, however far apart. No signal travels faster than light.",
 "Bell's theorem showed that no local hidden-variable theory can match quantum correlations. Experiments, including loophole-free ones in 2015, agree with quantum mechanics.",
 "CHSH: |S|≤2 classically, quantum max 2√2≈2.83; singlet correlation E(a,b)=−cos(a−b).",
 "Exact within quantum theory; interpretation open.",
 em="schrodinger",ev="Aspect 1982|Loophole-free Bell tests 2015|Nobel Prize 2022",refs="UP3 7",ex="quantum_computing quantum_measurement")
n("tunneling","Quantum tunnelling","1",(1e-15,1e-8),"C1","Quantum mechanics",
 "A particle can pass through an energy barrier that classically it could not climb. Tunnelling makes the Sun shine, flash memory work and radioactive alpha decay happen.",
 "The chance falls exponentially with barrier width and particle mass, so electrons tunnel readily but cats do not.",
 "T≈e^{−2κa}, κ=√(2m(V₀−E))/ħ; Gamow factor for fusion e^{−2πη}.",
 "Wave-mechanical effect; WKB formula is an approximation.",
 em="schrodinger",ev="Scanning tunnelling microscope|Alpha decay lifetimes",refs="UP3 7; UP3 10",ex="fusion_stars radioactivity")
n("quantum_measurement","Measurement problem and interpretations","1",None,"C4","Quantum mechanics",
 "Quantum theory predicts probabilities extremely well, but why a single definite result appears when we measure is still debated. Several interpretations give identical predictions.",
 "Main options: Copenhagen (collapse on measurement), many-worlds (no collapse, branching), Bohmian pilot waves, objective collapse models, and relational or Bayesian readings.",
 "Born rule p_i=|⟨i|ψ⟩|²; decoherence ρ→Σ_i p_i|i⟩⟨i| suppresses off-diagonal terms on timescale τ_D.",
 "The formalism is C1; which interpretation is right is untested. Collapse models make slightly different predictions that are being constrained.",
 em="schrodinger entanglement",ev="Interference experiments|Bounds on collapse-model parameters",refs="UP3 7",
 fr="Interpretations make the same predictions in all tests so far~open|Objective collapse models are being tested~open")
n("decoherence","Decoherence and the quantum-classical boundary","1",(1e-10,1e-3),"C2","Quantum mechanics",
 "Interaction with surroundings quickly scrambles the delicate phase relations that make quantum effects visible. That is why everyday objects look classical.",
 "Larger and warmer systems decohere faster. This is also the main obstacle for quantum computers.",
 "τ_D≈τ_R(λ_dB/Δx)²; coherence times: superconducting qubits ~10⁻⁴ s, trapped ions >1 s.",
 "Explains apparent collapse but does not by itself solve the measurement problem.",
 em="schrodinger quantum_measurement",ev="Cavity QED (Haroche)|Molecular interferometry",refs="UP3 7",ex="quantum_computing")
# ---------- QFT / SM ----------
n("qed","Quantum electrodynamics","1",(1e-18,1e-9),"C1","Quantum field theory",
 "QED describes light and charged matter as quantum fields exchanging photons. It is the most precisely tested theory in science.",
 "The electron's magnetic moment calculated from QED matches experiment to about one part in a trillion. Virtual particles are bookkeeping for terms in the calculation.",
 "L=ψ̄(iγ^μD_μ−m)ψ−¼F_μνF^μν; α=e²/(4πε₀ħc)≈1/137.036; a_e=(g−2)/2=0.00115965218.",
 "Perturbative in α; incomplete without weak and strong forces.",
 em="special_relativity schrodinger maxwell",ev="Lamb shift|Electron g-2",refs="UP3 11",ex="quantum_orbitals chemical_bonding")
n("standard_model","The Standard Model of particle physics","1",(1e-19,1e-15),"C2","Particle physics",
 "The Standard Model lists the known elementary particles (quarks, leptons, force carriers and the Higgs) and three of the four forces. It is tested to extraordinary precision but is known to be incomplete.",
 "It has 12 matter particles in three generations and gauge bosons for the strong, weak and electromagnetic forces. It omits gravity, dark matter and neutrino masses as normally written.",
 "Gauge group SU(3)_C×SU(2)_L×U(1)_Y; ~19 free parameters (more with neutrino masses); m_W=80.38 GeV, m_Z=91.19 GeV, m_H≈125 GeV.",
 "Valid up to at least TeV energies; new physics must exist for gravity and dark matter.",
 em="group_symmetry qed qcd",ev="LEP precision electroweak data|Higgs discovery 2012",refs="UP3 11",ex="quarks_leptons nuclear_force higgs_mechanism",sim="quark electron",fref="muon_g2")
n("quarks_leptons","Quarks and leptons","1",(1e-19,1e-16),"C1","Particle physics",
 "Ordinary matter is built from up and down quarks and electrons. Heavier copies of these exist in two more generations and are produced in cosmic rays and accelerators.",
 "Quarks come in six flavours with fractional charge; leptons include the electron, muon, tau and three neutrinos. Quarks are never seen alone.",
 "Charges: u +2/3, d −1/3, e −1; m_e=0.511 MeV, m_μ=105.66 MeV, m_τ=1776.9 MeV; m_t≈172.7 GeV.",
 "Point-like down to <10⁻¹⁸ m; why three generations is unknown.",
 em="standard_model",ev="Deep inelastic scattering|Discovery of top quark 1995",refs="UP3 11",ex="protons_neutrons",sim="quark electron",fr="Why there are three generations~open")
n("qcd","Quantum chromodynamics and confinement","1",(1e-16,1e-15),"C1","Particle physics",
 "QCD is the theory of the strong force, in which quarks carry 'colour' and exchange gluons. The force does not weaken with distance, so quarks are locked inside protons and neutrons.",
 "Most of a proton's mass comes from the energy of this strongly interacting field, not the quark masses. At very short distances quarks behave almost freely (asymptotic freedom).",
 "L=−¼G^a_μνG^{aμν}+Σq̄(iD̸−m)q; α_s(M_Z)=0.118; string tension ≈1 GeV/fm; m_p=938.27 MeV/c².",
 "Perturbative at high energy; non-perturbative regime solved numerically (lattice QCD).",
 em="group_symmetry special_relativity",ev="Jets at colliders|Lattice QCD hadron masses",refs="UP3 11",ex="protons_neutrons nuclear_force",sim="gluon proton",fr="Rigorous proof of the mass gap is missing~open")
n("higgs_mechanism","Higgs field and electroweak symmetry breaking","1",(1e-19,1e-17),"C1","Particle physics",
 "A field filling space gives mass to W and Z bosons and to the fundamental matter particles. The Higgs boson, found in 2012, is a ripple of that field.",
 "It explains why the weak force is short-range, but not why particle masses have their pattern. Only a small part of everyday mass comes from the Higgs.",
 "⟨φ⟩=v/√2, v=246 GeV; m_W=gv/2; m_f=y_f v/√2; m_H≈125.1 GeV.",
 "Higgs couplings agree with the Standard Model within ~10–20 %.",
 em="standard_model symmetry_breaking",ev="ATLAS and CMS 2012|Coupling measurements",refs="UP3 11",fr="Why the Higgs mass is so light (hierarchy problem)~open")
n("neutrinos","Neutrinos and their masses","1",(1e-19,1e26),"C2","Particle physics",
 "Neutrinos are nearly massless particles that barely interact. Billions pass through each person every second, and they change type (flavour) as they travel.",
 "Flavour oscillation proves they have mass, which the original Standard Model lacked. Their absolute mass scale and whether they are their own antiparticles remain unknown.",
 "P(ν_μ→ν_e)=sin²2θ sin²(1.27Δm²L/E); Δm²₂₁≈7.5×10⁻⁵ eV², |Δm²₃₁|≈2.5×10⁻³ eV²; Σm_ν<~0.1 eV from cosmology.",
 "Oscillation parameters well measured; ordering and absolute scale open.",
 em="standard_model",ev="Super-Kamiokande 1998|SNO 2001 solar deficit|Reactor experiments",refs="UP3 11; AST 16",fref="katrin_neutrino juno_oscillation",
 fr="Mass ordering unknown~open|Dirac versus Majorana nature unknown~open")
n("antimatter_asymmetry","Antimatter and the matter-antimatter imbalance","1",(1e-18,1e26),"C3","Particle physics",
 "Every particle has an antiparticle, yet the universe contains almost only matter. The Standard Model's tiny CP violation cannot explain the imbalance.",
 "Sakharov listed three conditions for creating a net excess: baryon-number violation, C and CP violation, and departure from equilibrium. Leptogenesis and electroweak baryogenesis are leading ideas.",
 "η=(n_B−n_B̄)/n_γ≈6×10⁻¹⁰; Jarlskog invariant J≈3×10⁻⁵.",
 "SM CP violation gives η too small by orders of magnitude.",
 em="standard_model big_bang",ev="Kaon and B-meson CP violation|CMB baryon fraction",refs="UP3 11",fref="lhcb_baryon_cp",fr="Mechanism of baryogenesis unknown~open",sim="cosmos")
n("strong_cp_axion","Strong CP problem and axions","1",(1e-19,1e-15),"C3","Particle physics",
 "QCD allows a CP-violating term, yet the neutron's electric dipole moment is tiny. The axion is a proposed particle that would explain it and could also be dark matter.",
 "Axion searches use strong magnetic fields to convert them into microwave photons.",
 "|θ̄|<10⁻¹⁰ (from d_n<1.8×10⁻²⁶ e cm); axion mass m_a≈5.7 μeV (10¹²GeV/f_a).",
 "Hypothetical; no detection yet.",
 em="qcd",ev="Neutron EDM limits|Axion haloscope limits",refs="UP3 11",fr="Axion not detected~open")
n("symmetry_breaking","Spontaneous symmetry breaking","1",None,"C1","Physics",
 "A system can have symmetric laws yet settle into a lopsided state, like a pencil balanced on its tip falling in some direction. This explains magnets, superconductors and how some particles get mass.",
 "Goldstone modes appear when a continuous symmetry breaks; in gauge theories they are absorbed to give mass (Higgs mechanism).",
 "V(φ)=−μ²|φ|²+λ|φ|⁴; ⟨φ⟩=μ/√(2λ)≠0.",
 "Applies from condensed matter to particle physics.",
 em="group_symmetry phase_transitions",ev="Ferromagnets|Superconductors|Higgs",refs="UP3 9",ex="higgs_mechanism superconductivity")
n("quantum_gravity","Quantum gravity","1",(1e-35,1e-34),"C4","Fundamental physics",
 "General relativity and quantum theory clash where gravity becomes strong at tiny scales, around the Planck length. No tested theory yet unites them.",
 "Main programmes are string theory, loop quantum gravity, asymptotic safety and causal sets. Tests would need tiny effects such as gravitational-wave echoes or lab entanglement via gravity.",
 "ℓ_P=√(ħG/c³)=1.616×10⁻³⁵ m; t_P=5.39×10⁻⁴⁴ s; E_P=1.22×10¹⁹ GeV.",
 "Speculative; no experiment yet probes ℓ_P.",
 em="general_relativity quantum_measurement standard_model",ev="Consistency arguments only",refs="UP3 11",ex="black_hole_info",fr="No experimentally tested theory exists~open")
n("black_hole_info","Black hole information paradox","1",(1e-10,1e13),"C3","Fundamental physics",
 "Hawking's calculation suggested black holes destroy information, clashing with quantum theory. Recent calculations suggest information escapes in subtle correlations.",
 "The 'Page curve' and 'island' calculations point to unitary evolution, but a full mechanism is not settled.",
 "S_BH=A/4ℓ_P²; evaporation time t≈5120πG²M³/(ħc⁴).",
 "Theoretical; requires quantum gravity.",
 em="black_holes quantum_gravity",ev="AdS/CFT consistency",refs="AST 24",fr="Mechanism of information recovery debated~open")
# ---------- nuclear ----------
n("protons_neutrons","Protons and neutrons","2",(1e-15,1e-15),"C1","Nuclear physics",
 "Protons and neutrons are each made of three quarks held by gluons, with radius about 0.84 femtometres. The neutron is slightly heavier and decays outside nuclei.",
 "Most of their mass is energy of the strong field. Measurements of the proton radius from muonic hydrogen and electron scattering have largely converged.",
 "m_p=938.272 MeV/c², m_n=939.565 MeV/c²; r_p≈0.84 fm; τ_n≈879 s (bottle/beam values differ by ~9 s).",
 "Composite particles; probe energies above ~1 GeV resolve quarks.",
 em="quarks_leptons qcd",ev="Electron scattering (Hofstadter)|Deep inelastic scattering",refs="UP3 11; UP3 10",ex="nuclear_force",sim="proton neutron quark",fr="Neutron lifetime discrepancy between methods~open")
n("nuclear_force","Nuclear force and nuclear structure","2",(1e-15,1e-14),"C1","Nuclear physics",
 "A short-range attraction between nucleons holds protons and neutrons together against electric repulsion. Nuclei are about 10,000 times smaller than atoms.",
 "Stable nuclei have a balance of neutrons and protons, with magic numbers (2, 8, 20, 28, 50, 82, 126) of extra stability.",
 "R=r₀A^{1/3}, r₀≈1.2 fm; nuclear density ≈2.3×10¹⁷ kg/m³; range ~1–2 fm.",
 "Effective description of residual QCD forces.",
 em="qcd protons_neutrons",ev="Rutherford scattering|Mass spectrometry",refs="UP3 10; CHE 21",ex="nuclear_binding radioactivity",sim="nucleus")
n("nuclear_binding","Nuclear binding energy","2",(1e-15,1e-14),"C1","Nuclear physics",
 "The mass of a nucleus is less than the sum of its parts; the missing mass is the energy that binds it. Binding per nucleon peaks near iron, so both fusion of light nuclei and fission of heavy ones release energy.",
 "The semi-empirical mass formula captures the balance of volume, surface, electric repulsion and pairing effects.",
 "B/A peaks ≈8.8 MeV at ⁵⁶Fe/⁶²Ni; B=a_vA−a_sA^{2/3}−a_cZ²/A^{1/3}−a_a(A−2Z)²/A±δ; fission of U-235 ≈200 MeV.",
 "Liquid-drop model is approximate; shell effects matter near magic numbers.",
 em="nuclear_force special_relativity",ev="Mass-spectrometer tables",refs="UP3 10; CHE 21",ex="fission_power fusion_stars nucleosynthesis_stars")
n("radioactivity","Radioactive decay and half-life","2",(1e-15,1e-10),"C1","Nuclear physics",
 "Unstable nuclei decay at random but with a fixed average rate, shown by a half-life. Alpha, beta and gamma are the main types.",
 "Alpha decay is tunnelling; beta decay changes a neutron into a proton via the weak force. Half-lives range from microseconds to billions of years.",
 "N=N₀e^{−λt}, t_{1/2}=ln2/λ; ¹⁴C 5730 y; ²³⁸U 4.468 Gy; ⁴⁰K 1.25 Gy.",
 "Statistical; individual decay times unpredictable.",
 em="nuclear_force tunneling weak_force_note",ev="Geiger counters|Radiometric dating",refs="UP3 10; CHE 21",ex="radiometric_dating geodynamo")
n("weak_force_note","Weak interaction","1",(1e-18,1e-15),"C1","Particle physics",
 "The weak force changes one type of quark or lepton into another. It drives beta decay and the first step of fusion in the Sun.",
 "It is carried by massive W and Z bosons, which makes it short-ranged and weak at low energy. It violates mirror symmetry (parity).",
 "G_F=1.166×10⁻⁵ GeV⁻²; n→p+e⁻+ν̄ₑ; Wu experiment 1957 (parity violation).",
 "Unified with electromagnetism above ~100 GeV.",
 em="standard_model higgs_mechanism",ev="Beta decay|Neutrino interactions|W/Z discovery 1983",refs="UP3 11; UP3 10")
n("radiometric_dating","Radiometric dating","8",(1e7,1e17),"C1","Geochronology",
 "Counting how much of a radioactive isotope has decayed into its product dates rocks and fossils. Different isotope pairs cover timescales from thousands to billions of years.",
 "Uranium-lead dates zircon crystals; carbon-14 dates organic remains back to ~50,000 years. Meteorites give Earth's age.",
 "t=(1/λ)ln(1+D/P); age of Earth 4.54±0.05 Gyr (Pb-Pb); oldest zircons ≈4.4 Gyr.",
 "Needs a closed system and known starting ratios.",
 em="radioactivity",ev="Concordia diagrams|Meteorite isochron",refs="CHE 21; AST 14",ex="deep_time")
n("fission_power","Nuclear fission and reactors","7",(1e-15,1e1),"C1","Nuclear technology",
 "A heavy nucleus such as uranium-235 splits after absorbing a neutron, releasing about 200 MeV and more neutrons that sustain a chain reaction.",
 "Reactors control the chain with absorbers and moderators and use the heat to make steam. Waste remains radioactive for long times.",
 "n+²³⁵U→fragments+2.4 n+~200 MeV; k_eff=1 critical; 1 kg ²³⁵U fission ≈8×10¹³ J.",
 "Engineering is mature; economics and waste policy are debated.",
 em="nuclear_binding",ev="Operating reactors since 1951",refs="UP3 10; CHE 21",ex="power_grid")
n("fusion_stars","Nuclear fusion","2",(1e-15,1e9),"C1","Nuclear physics",
 "Light nuclei combine and release energy when pushed together at extreme temperatures. Fusion powers the Sun and all stars.",
 "Protons must tunnel through electric repulsion; this makes fusion slow and steady in the Sun. In reactors deuterium and tritium fuse more easily.",
 "pp chain: 4¹H→⁴He+2e⁺+2ν+26.7 MeV; D+T→⁴He(3.5 MeV)+n(14.1 MeV); ~0.7 % of mass converted.",
 "Power-plant fusion not yet achieved; ignition demonstrated at NIF in 2022 in a lab shot.",
 em="nuclear_binding tunneling",ev="Solar neutrinos|Stellar luminosities|NIF 2022",refs="AST 16; UP3 10",ex="sun_structure nucleosynthesis_stars fusion_energy",sim="sun")
# ---------- atoms ----------
n("quantum_orbitals","Atomic structure and orbitals","3",(1e-11,1e-9),"C1","Atomic physics",
 "Electrons in atoms occupy fuzzy regions called orbitals, each with a fixed energy. Atoms emit or absorb light only when electrons jump between levels.",
 "Orbitals are described by quantum numbers n, l, m and spin. Orbital shape (s, p, d, f) determines bond directions in molecules.",
 "E_n=−13.606 eV/n²; Bohr radius a₀=0.0529 nm; ΔE=hf; shells hold 2n² electrons.",
 "Exact for hydrogen; multi-electron atoms require approximations (Hartree-Fock, DFT).",
 em="schrodinger spin_pauli qed",ev="Hydrogen and helium spectra|X-ray photoelectron spectra",refs="UP3 8; CHE 6; CAF 3",ex="periodic_table atomic_spectra chemical_bonding",sim="atom orbital electron")
n("atomic_spectra","Atomic spectra and lasers","3",(1e-9,1e-5),"C1","Atomic physics",
 "Each element emits and absorbs light at its own set of wavelengths, a fingerprint. Lasers make coherent light by stimulating many atoms to emit together.",
 "Spectra reveal what stars are made of. Stimulated emission with population inversion produces laser light, used from surgery to fibre communications.",
 "1/λ=R_H(1/n₁²−1/n₂²), R_H=1.097×10⁷ m⁻¹; Einstein A, B coefficients; laser gain requires N₂>N₁.",
 "Fine and hyperfine structure add small splittings.",
 em="quantum_orbitals electromagnetic_waves",ev="Fraunhofer lines|Helium discovered in the Sun",refs="UP3 8; AST 5",ex="stellar_spectra fiber_optics optical_clocks")
n("light_scattering","Scattering, absorption and sky colour","3",(1e-9,1e5),"C1","Atomic physics",
 "Air molecules scatter short wavelengths far more than long ones, so the sky is blue and sunsets are red. Clouds look white because droplets scatter all colours equally.",
 "Rayleigh scattering applies when particles are far smaller than the wavelength; larger drops give Mie scattering.",
 "Rayleigh I∝λ⁻⁴; blue (450 nm) scatters ~5.5× more than red (700 nm).",
 "Single-scattering limit; multiple scattering in thick clouds.",
 em="electromagnetic_waves quantum_orbitals",ev="Sky polarisation measurements",refs="UP3 1; AST 5",sim="air cloud")
n("periodic_table","The periodic table","3",(1e-11,1e-9),"C1","Atomic physics",
 "Elements arranged by atomic number repeat their chemical behaviour in a periodic pattern, because electron shells fill in a regular order.",
 "Elements in the same column have the same outer electron count. Trends in size, ionisation energy and electronegativity follow from effective nuclear charge.",
 "Aufbau order 1s 2s 2p 3s 3p 4s 3d 4p…; Z_eff=Z−S; first ionisation energy H 13.6 eV, He 24.6 eV, Na 5.14 eV.",
 "Aufbau has exceptions (Cr, Cu); heavy elements show relativistic effects (gold colour).",
 em="quantum_orbitals spin_pauli",ev="Mendeleev's predictions (Ga, Ge)|Spectroscopy",refs="CHE 6; CAF 3; AST 5",ex="chemical_bonding nucleosynthesis_stars",sim="atom")
n("optical_clocks","Atomic clocks and tests of constants","3",(1e-10,1e-9),"C2","Atomic physics",
 "Optical atomic clocks count light cycles from atoms and are accurate to about one second in the age of the universe. They test whether constants drift and may detect dark matter.",
 "The SI second is defined by caesium-133 hyperfine transition at 9 192 631 770 Hz.",
 "Fractional uncertainty ~10⁻¹⁸; Δν/ν sensitivity to α-variation; Sr lattice at 429 THz.",
 "No variation of constants found within current limits.",
 em="atomic_spectra",ev="Comparisons of Sr, Yb and Al⁺ clocks",refs="UP3 8",ex="gps_relativity")
n("antihydrogen","Antihydrogen","3",(1e-10,1e-9),"C2","Atomic physics",
 "Antimatter atoms made at CERN have the same spectrum as hydrogen so far, and tests of their weight show they fall down like normal matter.",
 "Studying antihydrogen tests CPT symmetry and the equivalence principle for antimatter.",
 "1S-2S transition measured to ~2×10⁻¹² relative precision, consistent with hydrogen.",
 "Few atoms available per experiment.",
 em="antimatter_asymmetry quantum_orbitals",ev="ALPHA and ATRAP experiments",refs="UP3 11")
